  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier with more seasonable temperatures today \nthrough much of this week.\n\nHigher dewpoints with light winds and wet surfaces have aided in the \ndevelopment of areas of fog this morning, particularly in areas that \ntend to be more prone to it. This is expected to burn off shortly \nafter sunrise.\n\nA cold front associated with low pressure centered over eastern \nOntario/western Quebec continues to track east over New England into \nthe morning hours today. In its wake, clearer skies and breezy \nsouthwesterly winds prevail over southern New England. Occasional \ngusts to around 20-25 MPH are possible in the afternoon. \nTemperatures today return to more seasonable levels with highs back \nin the upper 70s and low 80s. This general pattern will continue at \nleast into Wednesday as high pressure slowly builds in behind the \nfront.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return Thursday/Friday, though drier \nconditions look to return heading into the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure looks to shift offshore heading into Thursday ahead of \na shortwave over the Great Lakes. Slightly more agreement amongst \nthe ensembles regarding the track of its associated low pressure has \ncome into the picture with the latest model runs, but uncertainty \nstill remains. Low pressure develops over the Great Lakes, which \nguidance also resolves as extending up into Hudson Bay on the front \nend of the upper level trough. This low pressure is slated to track \neast through Canada, bringing showers down through New England. \nTiming-wise, guidance continues to key in on late Thursday/early \nFriday as the onset time for rain to return to southern New England, \nlasting through Friday. NAEFS guidance has continued to show more \nanomalous PWATs returning over the region within this timeframe as \nwell. This will very likely change to some degree in the coming days \nas we approach this event.\n\nFollowing rain towards the end of the work week, there are \nindications that high pressure makes a return heading into Saturday, \ndrying out southern New England once more.",
